When you need to "branch on one value" (say the command is start, stop or status, each doing something different), case is clearer than a long chain of if/elif:
  case "$1" in
    start)  echo ...;;
    stop)   echo ...;;
    *)      echo ...; exit 2;;
  esac
Each branch starts with ) and ends with ;;; * means "everything else" (the default branch); esac is case backwards, marking the end.
